{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NdHf4GRJ3pZpbcQb1L4h1A","type":"tool_result","content":"1	# Mathematical working context
2	
3	Treat candidates as unproved. Reprove every fact used in the final argument. Constraints below are hard stops: do not walk them again.
4	
5	## Frontier obligations
6	
7	- Effect of the connecting map from Gr^W_26 H^{24} of the weight-two quotient of the mixed R^1 local system on Gr^W_26 H^{25} of the weight-one symplectic part for M_{6,6} -> M_{6,5}.
8	- The (5,8) case: the only Gysin source into H^{26}(Mbar_{6,6}) from the non-separating boundary is Mbar_{5,8}, whose corresponding lowest-weight group is itself undischarged.
9	- Exhaustive component, dimension, stabilizer, closure, and codimension analysis of the evaluation-obstruction locus Z, including exclusion of larger components over special curve loci and distinction of component existence from purity.
10	- Constancy of h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 on the balanced locus, equivalently exclusion of balanced non-primitive degree-4 pencils.
11	- Monomorphic embedding of the balanced model into M_{6,6}: recovery of the tetragonal pencil from L, a well-defined source and target, and stack-level full faithfulness covering automorphism groups.
12	- Odd-tensor virtual-cohomological-dimension vanishing on M_{6,1} required by the five-stage forgetful Leray reduction of the exact target.
13	- A lowest-weight Tate theorem, or equivalent, for the Casnati-Ekedahl-surjective open U needed by localization arguments.
14	- Identification of H^{16}(Mbar_{6,6};Q) as a direct sum of Q(-8), including automorphism-invariant bookkeeping, Gysin shifts, differentials, and higher-genus primitives.
15	
16	## Mathematical constraints
17	
18	- Treating R^1 pi_* Q of the universal punctured curve as pure of weight 1 and using weight p as the floor at cohomological degree p. [reason: R^1 pi_* Q is an extension of a weight-2 Tate system by a weight-1 system, hence mixed of weights 1 and 2; the actual weight floor for H^p(base; L) is p+1, so a pure-weight-1 treatment is off by exactly one unit of weight and targets an identically zero group.]
19	- Identifying the top-weight graph-complex corner gr^W_0 H^*_c(M_{g,n}), equivalently gr^W_{2d}H^*(M_{g,n}), with the lowest-weight target gr^W_{2d-16}H^{2d-16}(M_{g,n})(d). [reason: The two corners coincide only if 2d = 2d-16, which never holds; for (6,6) the top-weight corner has weight 42 while the target has weight 26, and no twist or dimension shift reconciles distinct weights.]
20	- Bounding pure-weight cohomology of a local system on the total space from a gonality or Brill-Noether stratification of the base by evaluating only the open stratum. [reason: Such a bound requires the localization spectral sequence with codimension shifts and Tate twists (a codimension-4 stratum contributes through degree p-8 cohomology twisted by Q(-4)) and control of its differentials; restriction to the open stratum is in general neither injective nor surjective.]
21	- Normalizing the non-separating boundary divisor of Mbar_{6,6} by Mbar_{5,7}. [reason: Normalization of a non-separating node drops the genus by one and adds two legs at the node, giving 6+2 = 8 legs; dim Mbar_{5,7} = 19 is not 21-1 = 20, so Mbar_{5,7} cannot be the codimension-one boundary normalization.]
22	- Identifying a relative linear system with Proj(pi_*L) without qualification when h^0(L) jumps. [reason: Proj(pi_*L) has the correct fibres only over the open where h^0(L) is constant; a global identification requires cohomology-and-base-change hypotheses or a compatible divisor/Hilbert or Abel-Jacobi construction.]
23	- Concluding scheme-theoretic equality of the evaluation-obstruction locus and an Abel-Jacobi incidence locus from a fibrewise equivalence between nonvanishing of an obstruction space and triviality of a degree-zero line bundle. [reason: A fibrewise equivalence establishes equality only on geometric points; scheme-theoretic equality requires comparison of the determinantal/Fitting scheme structures.]
