  "argument_markdown": "## Argument\n\n### 0. Position relative to the working context\n\nThe primary working-context file lists as **frontier obligations** (open gaps, not inputs):\n\n1. \"An explicit scheme-theoretic proof that J subset I_e on Pic^0 over arbitrary (possibly nonreduced) locally Noetherian bases, via restriction of Phi along e and vanishing of Fit_0(coker(e^* Phi))=Fit_0(R^1 pi_* omega_{C/S}).\"\n2. \"Independent scheme-structure comparison establishing Z_CE=a_N^{-1}(e(S)) as closed subschemes (both inclusions of Fitting ideal versus section ideal), not merely fibrewise geometric-point equality.\"\n3. \"Justification that the balanced CE bundle ... either splits as W_4 oplus W_5 or that the Fitting reduction is rewritten in filtered form so that the split hypothesis is not required.\"\n4. \"Smooth-local/atlas descent extending the scheme-base CE/Abel-Jacobi identification to the corresponding algebraic stack.\"\n\nThe extended working-context file records the corresponding statements only as **candidate ideas requiring proof** (WORKING-EXT lines 69-71), and there under the *stronger* hypotheses of a characteristic-zero base and a **split** bundle W = W_4 oplus W_5. Packet finding FND-0046 and the extended-file constraints at lines 21 and 33 record that fibrewise geometric-point equality does not suffice and that a Fitting-ideal comparison is required. Nothing in either file proves the statement.\n\nThis attempt proves it, under strictly weaker hypotheses than the candidate: arbitrary characteristic, arbitrary (possibly nonreduced) locally Noetherian base, and only a filtration rather than a splitting. Packet findings FND-0096, FND-0097 and FND-0099 are consistent with the result and are cited below only for orientation; **every step used in the proof is proved here from standard inputs**, so no corroborated-finding sketch is load-bearing.\n\n### 1. Standard inputs used (textbook, not campaign-specific)\n\n- **(S1)** Grothendieck representability of the relative Picard functor. Since n >= 1, O_C(Gamma) is pi-ample, so pi is projective; with geometrically integral fibres and the section x_1, Pic_{C/B} is representable by a separated B-scheme locally of finite type, Pic^0_{C/B} is open and closed in it, smooth and proper over B of relative dimension g with identity section e, and a Poincare bundle Poin exists on C x_B Pic_{C/B}, unique up to twist by an invertible sheaf pulled back from Pic. [FGA Explained 9.4.8; BLR, Neron Models, 8.2 and 9.4.]\n- **(S2)** A section of a smooth morphism is a regular immersion (in particular a closed immersion when the morphism is separated), and I_e/I_e^2 is canonically e^*Omega^1_{Pic^0/B}, locally free of rank g; its formation commutes with base change B' -> B.\n- **(S3)** Cohomology and base change. For a proper morphism with 1-dimensional fibres, R^2 = 0, hence R^1 commutes with **arbitrary** base change. If moreover R^1 f_*F = 0 for F flat over the base, then f_*F is locally free and commutes with arbitrary base change.\n- **(S4)** Relative duality trace isomorphism R^1 pi_* omega_{C/B} = O_B for a smooth proper curve fibration with geometrically connected fibres.\n- **(S5)** Serre duality over a field kappa: for C smooth proper geometrically connected over kappa, the cup pairing H^0(omega) x H^1(O) -> H^1(omega) = kappa is perfect, and H^0(O_C) = kappa.\n- **(S6)** Fitting formalism. Fit_0(M) is defined for coherent M, independent of presentation; Fit_0(f^*M) = f^{-1}Fit_0(M).O_T for arbitrary f; Fit_0(M tensor L) = Fit_0(M) for L invertible; if E -> F -> M -> 0 with E, F locally free and rk F = r then Fit_0(M) = I_r(matrix); Fit_0(invertible) = 0; Fit_0(O_T/I) = I. [Eisenbud, Comm. Alg., 20.2 and Cor. 20.5.]\n- **(S7)** Deformation theory of line bundles: ker(Pic(C_{kappa[eps]}) -> Pic(C_kappa)) = H^1(C_kappa, O), the class xi corresponding to N_xi being the class of the extension 0 -> O -> N_xi -> O -> 0 given by multiplication by eps (up to a universal sign), and every xi arises this way.\n- **(S8)** Nakayama's lemma for finitely generated modules over Noetherian local rings.\n\n**Campaign source input** (used only in Step 7): SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6, packet primary locator, giving the CE convention W_f = (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-f))|_C, deg(W_f|_C) = 2(2g-2+4.2) - 4f = 4g+12-4f, and the direct-sum decomposition of the obstruction space. Pinned reusable computations reproduced here: deg(W_5)=16, deg(W_4)=20 at g=6 [sha256 b66575beab63338ae9b30183b1bf17bf9315d5308b26cd662e8e1aa24b641495], the internal consistency check deg(O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C)=18 giving 36-4f [sha256 ed50d74f9c39186cafd19d67c763077316a04decb7036e0dfef05bf70c2d7a94], and deg(omega tensor W_f^vee(Gamma)) = 4f+n-2g-14 [sha256 867736be32f96a3057aa0f070b3b89ed69f0b5f373e4779cea97dc561f5f536a]. These pin arithmetic only; the universal statements below are proved, not inferred from them.\n\n### 2. Step 1: ranks, and the cokernel of the universal evaluation\n\nWork in the setting (Sigma), (F), (D1), (D2) of the theorem.\n\nFrom (D1), d_4 > 2g-2+n > 2g-2; from (D2), d_5 = 2g-2+n > 2g-2 since n >= 1. Hence on every geometric fibre H^1(W_4) = H^1(W_5) = 0, so by (S3) R^1 pi_* W_4 = R^1 pi_* W_5 = 0 and, by the long exact sequence of (F), R^1 pi_* W = 0. Therefore pi_*W is locally free of rank chi(W) = d_4 + d_5 + 2(1-g) and commutes with arbitrary base change. Since Gamma -> B is finite locally free of degree n and W|_Gamma is locally free of rank 2 on Gamma, pi_*(W|_Gamma) is locally free of rank 2n and commutes with arbitrary base change. By (S6) this gives **(T1)**: Fit_0(coker phi) = I_{2n}(phi).\n\nNow the universal side. Put P := Pic^0_{C/B}, p : C_P -> P, Gamma_P := Gamma x_B P, and L_univ := omega_{C_P/P}(Gamma_P) tensor Poin^{-1}, of fibrewise degree 2g-2+n > 2g-2. By (S3), R^1 p_* L_univ = 0, p_*L_univ is locally free of rank g+n-1 and commutes with arbitrary base change; likewise p_*(L_univ|_{Gamma_P}) is locally free of rank n. Let Phi be the evaluation and J := Fit_0(coker Phi) = I_n(Phi).\n\nFrom 0 -> L_univ(-Gamma_P) -> L_univ -> L_univ|_{Gamma_P} -> 0 and R^1p_*L_univ = 0 we obtain the exact sequence\n\n  p_*L_univ -> p_*(L_univ|_{Gamma_P}) -> R^1p_*(L_univ(-Gamma_P)) -> 0,\n\nhence the identification, used throughout,\n\n  **(2.1)  coker Phi = R^1 p_*(omega_{C_P/P} tensor Poin^{-1}),**\n\nsince L_univ(-Gamma_P) = omega_{C_P/P} tensor Poin^{-1}. By (S3) the right-hand side commutes with **arbitrary** base change.\n\n### 3. Step 2: J subset I_e (frontier obligation 1)\n\nWe compute e^*J. By (S6), e^*J is exactly the image of J in O_B = O_P/I_e, so\n\n  J subset I_e  if and only if  Fit_0(coker(e^*Phi)) = 0 in O_B.\n\nRestricting along e: C_P x_P B = C, and by the normalisation of the Poincare bundle e^*Poin = O_C tensor pi^*M_0 for some invertible M_0 on B. By (S3) the formation of p_*L_univ, of p_*(L_univ|_{Gamma_P}) and of the evaluation between them commutes with base change, so e^*Phi is the evaluation\n\n  pi_*(omega_{C/B}(Gamma) tensor pi^*M_0^{-1}) -> pi_*((omega_{C/B}(Gamma) tensor pi^*M_0^{-1})|_Gamma).\n\nUsing 0 -> omega_{C/B} -> omega_{C/B}(Gamma) -> omega_{C/B}(Gamma)|_Gamma -> 0 twisted by pi^*M_0^{-1}, the projection formula, and R^1pi_*(omega_{C/B}(Gamma) tensor pi^*M_0^{-1}) = 0 (fibrewise degree 2g-2+n > 2g-2), we get\n\n  **(3.1)  coker(e^*Phi) = R^1pi_*(omega_{C/B}) tensor M_0^{-1} = M_0^{-1}**\n\nby the trace isomorphism (S4). This is an **invertible** O_B-module, so by (S6)\n\n  Fit_0(coker(e^*Phi)) = Fit_0(R^1pi_*omega_{C/B} tensor M_0^{-1}) = Fit_0(O_B) = 0.\n\nHence e^*J = 0 and **J subset I_e**, over an arbitrary locally Noetherian B, with no reducedness and no characteristic hypothesis. This is precisely the route named in frontier obligation 1 (restriction of Phi along e; vanishing of Fit_0(coker(e^*Phi)) = Fit_0(R^1pi_*omega_{C/B})), and it uses **no** statement about the support V(J). The primary-file constraint forbidding the passage \"V(J) = e(S) set-theoretically, therefore J subset I_e\" is therefore not walked: the counterexample template given there (P = Spec k[eps][x], J = (x^2, eps)) is defeated because our J satisfies the strictly stronger vanishing e^*J = 0, which is an equality of ideals in O_B and not a statement about reduced supports.\n\n### 4. Step 3: I_e subset J, with the generation check over A = O_P/I_e\n\nThis is the inclusion the primary-file constraint declares **not** to follow from \"the tangent map is an isomorphism\" alone, absent a generation check over A/I_e. We supply the check.\n\n**(4a) Points outside e(B).** Let z in P with residue field kappa := kappa(z), lying over b in B. By (2.1) and (S3),\n\n  (coker Phi) tensor kappa = H^1(C_kappa, omega tensor N_z^{-1}),\n\nwhere N_z is the degree-0 line bundle on C_kappa classified by z. By (S5) this is dual to H^0(C_kappa, N_z), which is nonzero exactly when the degree-0 bundle N_z is trivial, i.e. exactly when z lies in e(B). Since Fit_0 of a finite-dimensional vector space vanishes iff that space is nonzero, we get: J.kappa(z) = 0 iff z in e(B). Hence for z not in e(B) we have J_z = O_{P,z} = (I_e)_z (the latter because e(B) is closed), so the two stalks agree. Note this paragraph is used **only** for points off e(B); it is not used to compare ideals along e(B).\n\n**(4b) The first-order computation at every point of B.** Fix b in B, put k := kappa(b) and z := e(b), so kappa(z) = k. Let R := O_{P,z} with maximal ideal m, and A := O_{B,b} = R/(I_e)_z. Let P_b := P x_B Spec k = Pic^0_{C_b/k}, a smooth k-scheme of dimension g with rational point z.\n\nLet xi in T_z P_b = H^1(C_k, O_{C_k}) be nonzero and let iota_xi : Spec k[eps] -> P_b subset P be the corresponding tangent vector. Since Pic(k[eps]) = 0, iota_xi^*Poin is the line bundle N_xi on C_{k[eps]} of (S7) with class xi. Base change of (2.1) along iota_xi (legitimate by (S3), R^1 commuting with arbitrary base change) gives\n\n  iota_xi^*(coker Phi) = coker(iota_xi^*Phi) = H^1(C_{k[eps]}, omega tensor N_xi^{-1}).\n\nWrite M := N_xi^{-1}; by (S7) it sits in 0 -> O_{C_k} -> M -> O_{C_k} -> 0 with class -xi, nonzero. Tensoring by omega := omega_{C_k/k} and taking cohomology,\n\n  H^0(omega) --delta--> H^1(omega) -> H^1(omega tensor M) -> H^1(omega) -> 0,\n\nwhere delta is cup product with -xi under H^0(omega) x H^1(O) -> H^1(omega). By (S5) that pairing is perfect and H^1(omega) = k is one-dimensional, so xi nonzero forces delta nonzero, hence surjective. Therefore H^1(omega tensor M) = H^1(omega) = k, of length 1 over k[eps]. A k[eps]-module of length 1 is k[eps]/(eps), whose Fit_0 is (eps). Consequently\n\n  **(4.1)  iota_xi^*J = Fit_0(coker(iota_xi^*Phi)) = (eps)  for every nonzero xi in T_z P_b.**\n\nOnly Serre duality **over the field k** is used; no duality over Artinian rings is invoked.\n\n**(4c) Translation into conormal generation.** By (S2), e is a regular immersion and I_e/I_e^2 is locally free of rank g over O_B, with formation commuting with base change; in particular (I_e/I_e^2) tensor_A k is canonically the cotangent space m'/m'^2 of P_b at z, of dimension g, and T_z P_b is its dual. For f in (I_e)_z we have f(z) = 0, so iota_xi^*(f) = eps.<xi, f mod I_e^2>. Thus (4.1) says exactly: no nonzero xi annihilates the image V of J in (I_e/I_e^2) tensor_A k. Since that space is finite-dimensional over k, V^perp = 0 forces\n\n  **(4.2)  V = (I_e/I_e^2) tensor_A k.**\n\n**(4d) Nakayama over A = O_P/I_e.** Let Q := (I_e/I_e^2)_z, a free A-module of rank g, and V' subset Q the A-submodule generated by the image of J_z. By (4.2), V' + m_A Q = Q; since Q is finitely generated over the Noetherian local ring A, Nakayama (S8) gives V' = Q, i.e.\n\n  **(4.3)  (I_e)_z = J_z + (I_e)_z^2.**\n\nThis is the generation check over A/I_e demanded by the constraint: (4.2) is fibrewise spanning at the single point z, and (4.3) is the resulting statement over A, obtained by an explicit Nakayama and not by fiat. Note also that (4b)-(4d) are carried out at **every** point b of B, including non-closed points, so no genericity or density input is used.\n\n**(4e) Nakayama over R.** Let Mmod := (I_e)_z / J_z, a finitely generated R-module. By (4.3), Mmod = (I_e)_z . Mmod, and (I_e)_z is contained in m, so Mmod = m.Mmod and Nakayama gives Mmod = 0, i.e. J_z = (I_e)_z.\n\nCombining (4a) and (4e), J_z = (I_e)_z for every z in P, so **J = I_e**, proving (T2). No reducedness, irreducibility or characteristic hypothesis was used anywhere.\n\n### 5. Step 4: the filtered CE reduction, without any splitting (frontier obligation 3)\n\nWe now return to phi and the filtration (F), and never assume that (F) splits.\n\n- The sequence (F) has locally free quotient W_5, so it is locally split as a sequence of O_C-modules; hence restricting to Gamma preserves exactness, and Gamma -> B is finite (hence affine), so pi_* is exact on Gamma. Thus the bottom row 0 -> pi_*(W_4|_Gamma) -> pi_*(W|_Gamma) -> pi_*(W_5|_Gamma) -> 0 is exact.\n- The top row 0 -> pi_*W_4 -> pi_*W -> pi_*W_5 -> 0 is exact because R^1pi_*W_4 = 0, which holds since d_4 > 2g-2 by (D1). (Per FND-0099 this vanishing must be cited explicitly and is not supplied by finiteness of Gamma; it is cited here.)\n- coker(phi_4) = 0: from 0 -> W_4(-Gamma) -> W_4 -> W_4|_Gamma -> 0 and R^1pi_*W_4 = 0 we get coker(phi_4) = R^1pi_*(W_4(-Gamma)); the fibrewise degree is d_4 - n > 2g-2 by (D1), so H^1 vanishes on every geometric fibre, and since R^1 commutes with arbitrary base change (S3) the sheaf R^1pi_*(W_4(-Gamma)) has vanishing fibres everywhere, hence vanishes by Nakayama.\n\nThe snake lemma applied to (phi_4, phi, phi_5) then gives an exact sequence ... -> coker phi_4 -> coker phi -> coker phi_5 -> 0 with coker phi_4 = 0, hence an isomorphism coker phi = coker phi_5 and therefore\n\n  **(5.1)  Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5),**\n\nwhich is (T3). Exactly as in Step 2, coker phi_5 = R^1pi_*(W_5(-Gamma)).\n\n### 6. Step 5 and 6: identification, base change, T-points, stacks\n\nSet N := omega_{C/B} tensor W_5^vee(Gamma). By (D2) its fibrewise degree is 2g-2-d_5+n = 0, and by pure line-bundle algebra\n\n  **(6.1)  W_5(-Gamma) = omega_{C/B} tensor N^{-1}.**\n\nLet a_N : B -> P be the classifying morphism of N, characterised by (id_C x a_N)^*Poin = N tensor pi^*M for some invertible M on B (using (S1) and the section x_1). Base-changing (2.1) along a_N, with (S3) and the projection formula,\n\n  a_N^*(coker Phi) = R^1pi_*(omega_{C/B} tensor N^{-1}) tensor M^{-1} = coker(phi_5) tensor M^{-1}.\n\nBy (S6) (base change of Fitting ideals; invariance under twist by an invertible sheaf) and by (5.1) and (T2),\n\n  Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5) = Fit_0(a_N^*coker Phi) = a_N^{-1}(J).O_B = a_N^{-1}(I_e).O_B,\n\nand a_N^{-1}(I_e).O_B is by definition the ideal of the scheme-theoretic preimage a_N^{-1}(e(B)). This proves **(T4)**:\n\n  **Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) as closed subschemes of B.**\n\nThis is an equality of ideals, obtained by comparing Fitting ideals; it is not deduced from agreement of geometric points, so the routes blocked by FND-0046 and by the constraints at WORKING-EXT lines 21/33 and primary-file entries on geometric-point agreement are not used.\n\n**Base change and T-points (T5).** Every construction above (pi_*, R^1pi_*, restriction to Gamma, Fit_0, Pic^0, e, a_N, and scheme-theoretic preimage) commutes with arbitrary base change T -> B for T locally Noetherian, and the hypotheses (Sigma), (F), (D1), (D2) are stable under base change. Applying the theorem over T:\n\n- If N|_{C_T} = pi_T^*M' is relatively trivial then coker(phi_T) = R^1pi_{T*}(omega_{C_T/T}) tensor M'^{-1} = M'^{-1} is invertible, so Fit_0(coker phi_T) = 0 and T -> B factors through Z_ev.\n- Conversely if T -> B factors through Z_ev then Fit_0(coker phi_T) = 0, so a_{N_T}^{-1}(e(T)) = T, i.e. a_{N_T} factors through e, i.e. N|_{C_T} is relatively trivial.\n\nSo **Z_ev represents the subfunctor of relative triviality of N**. Because this is a criterion on T-points expressed by a quasi-coherent ideal whose formation commutes with arbitrary base change, it transports to stacks with an explicit atlas argument (frontier obligation 4, for this statement only): let X be a locally Noetherian algebraic stack carrying the data (Sigma), (F), (D1), (D2), and u : U -> X a smooth surjection from a locally Noetherian scheme. The ideal Fit_0(coker phi) is a quasi-coherent ideal on X; its pullback to U and to U x_X U is the corresponding Fitting ideal there (base change), where the scheme-level theorem applies; and quasi-coherent sheaves satisfy smooth descent, so the closed substack Z_ev := V(Fit_0(coker phi)) is well defined and, by the T-point criterion applied to every scheme T -> X, represents relative triviality of N on X. No representability of Pic^0 over X is needed for this formulation. (The constraint forbidding stack transfer \"without an atlas or descent remark\" is thereby met by an actual descent argument, not a remark.)\n\n### 7. Step 7: the (6,6) balanced tetragonal instance (T6)\n\nLet gamma : C -> P^1_B be finite flat of degree 4 with C/B a smooth proper family of genus-6 curves, q : P^1_B -> B the structure map, and let F be the CE relations bundle, of rank 2 on P^1_B, assumed **balanced**: F_b = O(4) direct-sum O(5) on every geometric fibre.\n\n**Canonical filtration (proved here, not assumed).** Consider q_*(F(-5)). Fibrewise F(-5) = O(-1) direct-sum O(0), so h^0 = 1 and h^1 = 0 on every geometric fibre; by (S3), q_*(F(-5)) is invertible and commutes with base change, and the counit q^*q_*(F(-5)) -> F(-5) is fibrewise the inclusion of the O(0) summand into O(-1) direct-sum O(0). Twisting back, L_5 := O_{P^1}(5) tensor q^*q_*(F(-5)) -> F is a map of bundles which on every geometric fibre is the inclusion of the O(5) summand, hence has locally free cokernel L_4 (fibrewise O(4)); so\n\n  0 -> L_5 -> F -> L_4 -> 0\n\nis a canonical short exact sequence of bundles on P^1_B (the relative Harder-Narasimhan filtration; consistent with FND-0097). Dualising and tensoring by the invertible sheaf O_{P(E^vee)}(2)|_C, and using flatness of gamma so that gamma^* is exact, we obtain a canonical short exact sequence on C\n\n  0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0,  W := (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*F^vee)|_C,\n\nwith W_4 := (O(2) tensor gamma^*L_4^vee)|_C the **sub** and W_5 := (O(2) tensor gamma^*L_5^vee)|_C the **quotient**. Since L_5 is fibrewise O(5) and L_4 fibrewise O(4), and since twisting by q^*(invertible on B) has fibrewise degree 0, these agree fibrewise with the packet convention W_f = (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-f))|_C. In particular W_5 is built from F^vee with the gamma^*O(-5) twist and is **not** the O_{P^1}(5) summand of F, so the two sign/convention constraints recorded in the primary file (deg = 4g+12-4f requires gamma^*O(-f); W_5 is not the O(5) summand of F) are respected.\n\n**Degrees.** By SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6, deg(W_f|_C) = 2(2g-2+4.2) - 4f = 4g+12-4f. At g = 6 this gives d_4 = deg(W_4) = 4.6+12-16 = 20 and d_5 = deg(W_5) = 4.6+12-20 = 16 (internal check: deg(O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C) = 2g-2+8 = 18 and deg gamma = 4, so 2.18-4f = 36-4f).\n\n**Hypotheses verified at (g,n) = (6,6).** (D1): d_4 - n = 20 - 6 = 14 > 10 = 2g-2. (D2): d_5 - n = 16 - 6 = 10 = 2g-2. Also n = 6 >= 1 and g = 6 >= 1. By (T1), pi_*W is locally free of rank d_4+d_5+2-2g = 20+16+2-12 = 26 and pi_*(W|_Gamma) of rank 2n = 12, so Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi), matching the packet's 26 -> 12 bookkeeping.\n\n**Conclusion at (6,6).** N = omega_{C/B} tensor W_5^vee(Gamma) has fibrewise degree 10 - 16 + 6 = 0, and\n\n  **Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) as closed subschemes of B,**\n\nwith the T-point description: T -> B factors through Z_ev iff O_{C_T}(Gamma_T) = W_5|_{C_T} tensor omega_{C_T/T}^{-1} tensor pi_T^*M for some invertible M on T. On geometric points this recovers the packet's fibrewise criterion O_C(Gamma) = W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} (FND-0062, FND-0063), which is thereby upgraded from a geometric-point statement to an equality of ideals over arbitrary bases.\n\n**Note on genericity of the degree conditions.** (D1) and (D2) are the exact hypotheses that make the argument run, and they isolate what is special about (6,6): by the SRC-0004 formula the f=5 obstruction degree is 4f+n-2g-14 = n-6 at g=6, so (D2) holds precisely at n=6, while (D1) reads n < 10. This is the scheme-theoretic counterpart of FND-0074's observation that n=6 is the unique borderline value; consistently with FND-0096, the universal Pic^0 lemma (T2) itself is proved for all g >= 1, n >= 1 and is not (6,6)-specific, the specialisation entering only through (D2).\n\n### 8. Separation of status\n\n**Proved here (no undischarged dependency):** (T1)-(T6), from the standard inputs (S1)-(S8).\n\n**Source-backed input:** SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 (CE convention and degree formula), used only in Step 7 to identify d_4 = 20 and d_5 = 16 for the balanced tetragonal datum. If one prefers, Step 7 may be read as conditional on that convention; Steps 2-6 are unconditional.\n\n**Computational hypotheses:** none. The pinned reusable computations cited are finite arithmetic checks of 20, 16, 14, 10 and are reproved inline; no finite computation is used to support a universal claim.\n\n**Not claimed (explicit exclusions).** This artifact makes no assertion about, and its statement does not depend on, any of the following, each of which remains open:\n\n1. Nonemptiness of Z_ev, of its reduced-support open, or of the ordered Conf_6 incidence on geometric fibres (FND-0083; chi(L)=1 gives only nonemptiness of the full Sym^6 Abel-Jacobi fibre).\n2. The h^0 stratification of the relative degree-six linear system, and whether h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 on the whole balanced locus (FND-0058, FND-0073; extended-file constraint on the relative P^2-bundle claim).\n3. Dimension, component structure, purity, codimension, stabilizers, smoothness, closure, or lci-ness of Z_ev (FND-0060, FND-0068, FND-0069, FND-0086; extended-file constraints on b-3=15, on smoothness from a dimension count, and on codimension-4 Gysin).\n4. Any morphism, monomorphism, or comparison of Z_ev with a locus in M_{6,6}, and any recovery of the tetragonal pencil gamma from L (FND-0091, FND-0098). In particular the identification W_5 = omega_C^2(-D) (GAP-Z-W5D) is **not** used anywhere above.\n5. Any behaviour over unbalanced splitting types, non-reduced divisors, or jumping strata.\n6. Any Hodge-theoretic, weight, purity or Tate-type conclusion, and in particular anything about W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) or W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q). Those belong to C66-TATE-SUPPORT and C66-TATE-ASSEMBLY and are downstream of C66-GEO-H0 and C66-GEO-COMP.\n7. Integral-coefficient or coarse-space versions of the S_6 ordering comparison (FND-0075).\n\nThere are no gap markers because there are no clauses asserted above that the argument does not prove; items 1-7 are excluded from the statement rather than asserted with a caveat.\n\n### 9. Failed routes explicitly addressed\n\n- **\"From V(J) = e(S) set-theoretically conclude J subset I_e.\"** Not used. Step 3 proves e^*J = 0 by the Fitting base-change computation Fit_0(coker(e^*Phi)) = Fit_0(R^1pi_*omega_{C/B}) = 0. The k[eps][x] template that defeats the support argument does not apply, because e^*J = 0 is an equality of ideals in O_B.\n- **\"From 'the tangent map is an isomorphism' conclude that the first-order maximal minors generate I_e/I_e^2.\"** Not used. Step 4 replaces the bare tangent-space assertion by (4.1), a length computation of coker(iota_xi^*Phi) over k[eps] at every point of B, then performs the generation check over A = O_P/I_e by an explicit Nakayama (4.3), then a second Nakayama over O_{P,z} (4e). The WORKING-EXT candidate at line 70 sketches the corank-1 normal form and Schur complement; that normal form is not needed and is not used here.\n- **\"Identify Z_CE with the Abel-Jacobi incidence from agreement of geometric points.\"** Not used (FND-0046; extended-file constraints 21 and 33). Step 6 compares ideals. Geometric points appear only in (4a), for points **outside** e(B), where the comparison of stalks is between two unit ideals.\n- **\"Infer a global splitting W = W_4 direct-sum W_5 from the fibrewise balanced condition.\"** Not used. Step 5 uses only the filtration (F), and Step 7 constructs that filtration canonically from q_*(F(-5)); no Ext^1 vanishing over the base is needed.\n- **\"Exactness of pi_* from finiteness of Gamma.\"** Not used (FND-0099). Step 5 cites R^1pi_*W_4 = 0 (from d_4 > 2g-2) and R^1pi_*(W_4(-Gamma)) = 0 (from d_4-n > 2g-2) explicitly, and descends the latter from geometric fibres by base change plus Nakayama.\n- **\"Transfer a scheme-base identification to the universal stack without an atlas or descent remark.\"** Not used. Step 6 gives a T-point criterion and a smooth-descent argument on an explicit atlas.\n- **\"Identify the relative incidence space with Proj of pi_*L.\"** Not used (FND-0047; extended-file constraints 22 and 34). No linear-system model appears; Z_ev is defined by a Fitting ideal and identified with a scheme-theoretic preimage.\n- **\"Model the ordered configuration space by reducing only the source (I_red -> I^{red-supp}).\"** Not used (FND-0087). No reduction is applied to either side anywhere in this argument.\n- **\"Conclude nonemptiness of the reduced-support open or of Z^{geo} from chi(L) = 1.\"** Not used (FND-0083); nonemptiness is explicitly excluded.\n- **\"Call the reduced-support locus closed\" / \"treat Z as a closed pure-codimension-4 subvariety of M_{6,6}\".** Not used (FND-0052; extended-file constraint 28). Z_ev is a closed subscheme of the **parameter base B** of the family, not of M_{6,6}, and no codimension is asserted.\n- **Blocked campaign routes** vcd-only-vanishing, undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex, point-count-without-degree-parity-separation, forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown: none is used; no cohomological or weight-theoretic step occurs in this argument. Accordingly new_inputs is empty.\n\n### 10.