{"schema_version": 3, "id": "ATT-0063", "task_id": "TASK-C66-M-008", "campaign_id": "C66-001", "campaign_revision": 4, "subproblem_id": "C66-COMP-RANK", "lane": "computation", "result_type": "lemma", "target_claim_id": "RED-0001", "context_revision": 2, "packet_id": "C66-001-v4", "packet_path": "proof/packets/generated/C66-001-v4.md", "packet_sha256": "d61f334875795956d6abfac671fc0773a052ce6e9db62f2a31fffa904ecbb15c", "packet_binding_sha256": "76cc8a6d6ca02a2a9b1d7857fa11d7278f9732fa823f7be67dd51a354645bcc7", "target": {"chow_codimension": 13, "compact_cohomology_degree": 16, "compact_tate_type": "Q(-8)", "dimension": 21, "g": 6, "n": 6, "open_bm_degree": 16, "open_bm_tate_type": "Q(8)", "open_bm_weight": -16, "ordinary_cohomology_degree": 26, "ordinary_tate_type": "Q(-13)", "ordinary_weight": 26, "poincare_twist": 21}, "theorem_statement": "Let k be algebraically closed of characteristic zero. Let C be a smooth projective connected curve of genus 6 over k, gamma : C -> P^1 finite flat of degree 4, D := gamma^*(pt) of degree 4, and let (E,F) be the Casnati-Ekedahl data of gamma: gamma_*O_C = O_{P^1} + E^vee with E locally free of rank 3, pi : P := Proj Sym(E) -> P^1 the associated P^2-bundle with pi_*O_P(1) = E, C included in P the CE embedding, and F the rank-2 CE relations bundle, characterised equivalently by F = pi_*(I_C tensor O_P(2)). Write W_f := (O_P(2) tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-f))|_C (the SRC-0004 sign convention), W := (O_P(2) tensor pi^*F^vee)|_C, and call (C,gamma) balanced if F is isomorphic to O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5). Then: (A) O_P(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^*O(2), hence W_4 = omega_C^2 of degree 20, W_5 = omega_C^2(-D) of degree 16, and L := W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = omega_C(-D) of degree 6 with chi(L)=1 and h^1(L) = h^0(O_C(D)), so dim|L| = h^1(L). (B) h^0(O_C(D)) is 2 or 3, and h^0(O_C(D)) = 3 if and only if C is hyperelliptic, in which case D is linearly equivalent to 2H (H the hyperelliptic class), gamma factors as beta o h with h the hyperelliptic map and beta of degree 2, and E is isomorphic to O(1) + O(4) + O(4). (C) If C is hyperelliptic then h^0(F(-6)) >= 2, so F is not isomorphic to O(4)+O(5); consequently, if (C,gamma) is balanced then C is not hyperelliptic, h^0(O_C(D)) = 2, and h^0(L) = 3, h^1(L) = 2 exactly, so the residual linear system |L| is a P^2 for every balanced (C,gamma) with no jumping anywhere on the balanced locus. (D) For balanced (C,gamma) and every effective divisor Gamma of degree 6 on C, the evaluation phi_Gamma : H^0(W) -> H^0(W|_Gamma) (ranks 26 -> 12) has coker(phi_4) = 0 and coker(phi_Gamma) isomorphic to coker(phi_5) = H^1(W_5(-Gamma)), which is 1-dimensional if O_C(Gamma) is isomorphic to L and 0 otherwise; hence corank(phi_Gamma) <= 1 always, rk(phi_Gamma) is 11 or 12, and over any base B carrying a balanced family with a relative degree-6 divisor Gamma one has Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi) = I_6(phi_5) while I_11(phi) = O_B, i.e. the corank->=2 determinantal stratum of the CE evaluation is empty at (g,n)=(6,6). (E) Let B be a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme carrying a family (p : C_B -> B, gamma_B) of balanced pairs. Then p_*L_B is locally free of rank 3 and R^1p_*L_B is locally free of rank 2, both commuting with arbitrary base change; the class a_L : B -> Pic^6_{C_B/B} is canonical (independent of the Pic(B)-twist ambiguity of L_B); and the relative Abel-Jacobi incidence AJ_B := Sym^6(C_B/B) x_{Pic^6_{C_B/B}, a_L} B is canonically isomorphic to the Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle P(p_*L_B) over B. In particular AJ_B -> B is smooth and proper of pure relative dimension 2 and dim AJ_B = dim B + 2 on every component of B, and over the ordered pairwise-distinct locus AJ_B pulls back to the scheme-theoretic CE evaluation-failure locus Z_ev of ATT-0048. (F) For balanced (C,gamma) and Gamma in |L|, one has omega_C(-Gamma) isomorphic to O_C(D) with h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = 2, its complete linear system is base-point-free and coincides with the gamma-pencil; hence gamma is recovered from the pair (C,Gamma) uniquely up to PGL_2 acting on the target, the classifying map on geometric points is injective on isomorphism classes, and the forgetful homomorphism Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) -> Aut(C,Gamma) is a group isomorphism. (G) Consequently, for B as in (E) the image of the reduced-support open of AJ_B in M_{6,6} has dimension dim B + 2 and codimension 19 - dim B; in particular the codimension equals 4 if and only if dim B = 15, an equality which is not asserted here. (H) The excess of dim AJ_B over the generic determinantal expectation for the corank-1 locus of a 6-by-11 matrix equals h^1(L) = 2 exactly; no local complete intersection, smoothness, closedness, Gysin, purity-of-Hodge-structure, or Tate-type conclusion is drawn.", "theorem_scope": {"g": 6, "n": 6, "base_field": "algebraically closed, characteristic zero (Clifford equality case and the P^1 x P^1 bidegree argument are used; the relative statements additionally assume B reduced and locally Noetherian)", "generality": "Parts (A),(B) hold for every degree-4 cover of a genus-6 curve; parts (C)-(H) are stated for the balanced CE splitting type F = O(4)+O(5) at (g,n)=(6,6); nothing is claimed for unbalanced splitting types", "not_covered": "unbalanced splitting types; nonemptiness of the reduced-support/Conf_6 locus; base-point-freeness of L; dim B = 15; smoothness or local structure of the image in M_{6,6}; closedness in M_{6,6}; any weight, purity or Tate-type statement"}, "summary": "Computation-lane lemma for C66-COMP-RANK. Two things are computed exactly on the balanced tetragonal (6,6) locus: the fibre dimension of the residual linear system, and the determinantal rank-drop stratification of the CE evaluation. (i) Fibre dimension: h^0(L) = 1 + h^0(O_C(D)) with L = omega_C(-D); h^0(O_C(D)) is 3 exactly on hyperelliptic curves (Clifford equality one way; a P^1 x P^1 bidegree/adjunction argument the other way, showing a hyperelliptic genus-6 curve admits no base-point-free degree-4 pencil with h^0 = 2); and on a hyperelliptic curve E = O(1)+O(4)+O(4), whence h^0(F(-6)) >= 2 from the exact sequence 0 -> F -> Sym^2 E -> gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4)) -> 0, so F is not O(4)+O(5). Therefore balanced implies non-hyperelliptic implies h^0(L) = 3 and h^1(L) = 2 exactly, with no jumping on the balanced locus. This proves the hypothesis FND-0058 requires, and thereby licenses the relative Abel-Jacobi incidence over a balanced base to be a Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle of pure relative dimension 2, rather than assuming it. (ii) Rank drop: coker(phi) is isomorphic to coker(phi_5) = H^1(W_5(-Gamma)), which has dimension at most 1 always; so the 12-by-26 CE evaluation has corank at most 1, Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi) = I_6(phi_5), and I_11(phi) is the unit ideal, i.e. the deeper determinantal strata are empty. The excess of the actual fibre dimension 2 over the generic determinantal expectation 0 is identified as exactly h^1(L) = 2. Additionally gamma is recovered from (C,Gamma) as the complete pencil |omega_C(-Gamma)|, giving injectivity on geometric points and an isomorphism Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) = Aut(C,Gamma), which supplies the stabilizer comparison FND-0060 asks for and the pencil-recovery half of the monomorphic-embedding obligation. Codimension 4 in M_{6,6} is stated only as an implication conditional on dim B = 15, which is not asserted; no lci, smoothness, closedness, Gysin, nonemptiness, or Hodge/Tate claim is made.", "argument_markdown": "## Argument\n\n### 0. Setting, conventions, and what is imported\n\nThroughout, k is algebraically closed of characteristic 0 and \"curve\" means smooth projective connected over k. Cohomology is coherent cohomology; no topological or Hodge-theoretic notion occurs anywhere in this artifact.\n\n**Setting (T).** C is a curve of genus g = 6, gamma : C -> P^1 is finite flat of degree 4, and D := gamma^*(pt), an effective divisor of degree 4 whose class O_C(D) = gamma^*O_{P^1}(1) does not depend on the chosen point. Since char k = 0, gamma is separable. The Casnati-Ekedahl datum of gamma is: gamma_*O_C = O_{P^1} + E^vee with E locally free of rank 3 on P^1; pi : P := Proj Sym(E) -> P^1 the associated P^2-bundle, so pi_*O_P(1) = E and R^i pi_* O_P(m) = 0 for all i when m = -1 or m = -2; and C included in P the CE embedding, with relations bundle F locally free of rank 2 sitting in the CE resolution\n\n  (R)  0 -> pi^*(det E) tensor O_P(-4) -> pi^*F tensor O_P(-2) -> O_P -> O_C -> 0.\n\n**(N) Normalisation.** O_P(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(2).\n\n*Justification.* This is the SRC-0004 (Casnati-Ekedahl) normalisation, under which O_P(1)|_C is the relative dualising sheaf omega_{C/P^1} = omega_C tensor gamma^*omega_{P^1}^{-1} = omega_C tensor gamma^*O(2). It is also forced by the weaker form: if only O_P(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^*(O(2) tensor A) for some A in Pic(P^1) is granted, then taking degrees and using the SRC-0004 receipt deg(O_P(1)|_C) = 2g-2+4.2 = 18 [pinned reusable computation sha256 ed50d74f9c39186cafd19d67c763077316a04decb7036e0dfef05bf70c2d7a94] gives 10 + 8 + 4.deg A = 18, so deg A = 0, and Pic(P^1) = Z forces A = O_{P^1}. Hence (N). (This is the argument recorded in the extended working context as the \"weak CE normalization\" entry; it is reproved here rather than cited, because ATT-0048 records the resulting identification as the undischarged marker GAP-Z-W5D and deliberately avoids it.)\n\n**(W) CE summands.** Following SRC-0004 (and the packet's explicit sign instruction), set W_f := (O_P(2) tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-f))|_C and W := (O_P(2) tensor pi^*F^vee)|_C. By (N),\n\n  W_f = omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4-f),  deg W_f = 2.18 - 4f = 36 - 4f = 4g+12-4f at g = 6.\n\nHence deg W_4 = 20 and deg W_5 = 16, in agreement with SRC-0004 and with the pinned receipts sha256 b66575beab63338ae9b30183b1bf17bf9315d5308b26cd662e8e1aa24b641495 and 206494921a62c47555473286612852fce99ecb51c2b877f535a232e6b9cf0483. Explicitly\n\n  **(0.1)  W_4 = omega_C^2  and  W_5 = omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(-1) = omega_C^2(-D),**\n\nso that\n\n  **(0.2)  L := W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = omega_C(-D),  deg L = 16 - 10 = 6.**\n\nThe gamma^*O(-f) sign is the one the packet and the working context require; the opposite sign would give deg W_5 = 56 and destroy the degree-zero balance, and W_5 is emphatically not the O_{P^1}(5) summand of F. (0.1) discharges the marker GAP-Z-W5D.\n\n**Balancedness.** (C,gamma) is *balanced* if F is isomorphic to O_{P^1}(4) + O_{P^1}(5). This is the packet's canonical geometric bottleneck.\n\n**Imported dependency.** From ATT-0048 (subproblem C66-GEO-Z), whose claims are confirmed independently by REV-0091 and REV-0092, I use: CLM-0048-6 (over a balanced family the relative Harder-Narasimhan filtration yields a canonical exact sequence 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 with W_4 the sub, with no global splitting hypothesis); CLM-0048-3 (the filtered snake-lemma identity Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5)); CLM-0048-7 (at (6,6) the ranks are 26 -> 12 and Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi)); and CLM-0048-4/CLM-0048-5 (the scheme-theoretic identity Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) with its T-point criterion). In Step 4 below I re-derive the fibrewise content of CLM-0048-3 from scratch, so that the corank statement of Part (D) is self-contained on geometric fibres and only the *relative, scheme-theoretic* half leans on ATT-0048.\n\n**Source-backed input.** SRC-0004 (Casnati-Ekedahl), for the structure of the resolution (R), the rank-2 relations bundle F, the convention W_f, and the degree formula deg(W_f|_C) = 4g+12-4f. Everything else below is proved.\n\n### 1. Step 1: Riemann-Roch for the residual bundle; the fibre dimension is h^1(L)\n\nBy (0.2), deg L = 6 and g = 6, so Riemann-Roch gives\n\n  **(1.1)  chi(L) = deg L - g + 1 = 6 - 6 + 1 = 1,  h^0(L) = 1 + h^1(L).**\n\nBy Serre duality (used only for the identity h^1(L) = h^0(omega_C tensor L^{-1}), which is its legitimate use here; the degree-zero triviality criterion elsewhere in the campaign needs no duality),\n\n  h^1(L) = h^0(omega_C tensor L^{-1}) = h^0(omega_C tensor omega_C^{-1}(D)) = h^0(O_C(D)),\n\nso\n\n  **(1.2)  h^0(L) = 1 + h^0(O_C(D)),  and  dim |L| = h^0(L) - 1 = h^1(L) = h^0(O_C(D)).**\n\nThe second equality in (1.2) is the exact sense in which \"the fibre dimension of the residual linear system\" is a question about the degree-4 bundle O_C(D) and not about a degree-6 one: because chi(L) = 1, the projective dimension of |L| equals h^1(L) on the nose. (Consistently with the packet's M := omega_C^2 tensor W_5^vee: by (0.1), M = O_C(D), of degree 4, and (1.2) is the identity h^0(L) = 1 + h^0(M).)\n\n### 2. Step 2: the dichotomy h^0(O_C(D)) in {2,3}, with equality 3 exactly on hyperelliptic curves\n\n**(2a) Lower bound.** gamma is a morphism to P^1, so gamma^*H^0(P^1,O(1)) is a 2-dimensional base-point-free subspace of H^0(C,O_C(D)); hence h^0(O_C(D)) >= 2.\n\n**(2b) D is special.** h^1(O_C(D)) = h^0(omega_C(-D)) = h^0(L) >= chi(L) = 1 by (1.1). So D is a special effective divisor.\n\n**(2c) Upper bound and the equality case.** Clifford's theorem for special effective divisors gives h^0(O_C(D)) <= deg(D)/2 + 1 = 3, with equality only if D = 0, D = K_C, or C is hyperelliptic and D is linearly equivalent to a multiple of the hyperelliptic class H. Here deg D = 4, while deg 0 = 0 and deg K_C = 10, so equality forces C hyperelliptic and D linearly equivalent to 2H.\n\n**(2d) Converse: on a hyperelliptic genus-6 curve, h^0(O_C(D)) = 3 for every degree-4 cover.** Let C be hyperelliptic with hyperelliptic map h : C -> P^1, and suppose h^0(O_C(D)) = 2. Consider psi := (h,gamma) : C -> P^1 x P^1; both components are non-constant, so Sigma := psi(C) is an irreducible curve. Let delta := deg(C -> Sigma). Composing with the first projection, 2 = deg h = delta . deg(pr_1|_Sigma), so delta is 1 or 2.\n\n  * If delta = 2, then deg(pr_1|_Sigma) = 1, so pr_1 restricted to Sigma is birational and Sigma is rational; letting nu : P^1 -> Sigma be the normalisation, the map C -> Sigma lifts (C is smooth) to a degree-2 morphism C -> P^1, which must be h because a curve of genus >= 2 carries at most one g^1_2. Then pr_2 o nu : P^1 -> P^1 has degree deg(gamma)/2 = 2 and gamma = (pr_2 o nu) o h, so D = h^*((pr_2 o nu)^*(pt)) is linearly equivalent to h^*O(2) = 2H and h^0(O_C(D)) = h^0(2H) = 3, contradicting h^0(O_C(D)) = 2.\n  * If delta = 1, then psi is birational onto Sigma, so C is the normalisation of Sigma and g(C) <= p_a(Sigma). Write the class of Sigma as aA + bB with A = {pt} x P^1 and B = P^1 x {pt}, A.A = B.B = 0, A.B = 1. Then deg(pr_1|_Sigma) = Sigma.A = b = 2 and deg(pr_2|_Sigma) = Sigma.B = a = 4. Adjunction with K_{P^1 x P^1} = -2A-2B gives 2p_a(Sigma) - 2 = Sigma.(Sigma + K) = 2ab - 2a - 2b, i.e. p_a(Sigma) = (a-1)(b-1) = 3.1 = 3. But g(C) = 6 > 3, a contradiction.\n\nBoth cases are impossible, so h^0(O_C(D)) is not 2; by (2a) and (2c) it equals 3.\n\nCombining (2a)-(2d):\n\n  **(2.1)  h^0(O_C(D)) is 2 or 3; it equals 3 if and only if C is hyperelliptic, and then D is linearly equivalent to 2H.**\n\n(Only the direction \"h^0 = 3 implies hyperelliptic\" is needed for Part (C); the converse (2d) is what upgrades the conclusion to \"balanced implies non-hyperelliptic\", which is a genuine restriction on the balanced locus and is used in Step 6.)\n\n**(2e) The Tschirnhausen bundle in the hyperelliptic case.** Let C be hyperelliptic of genus 6 and gamma of degree 4. By (2.1), h^0(O_C(D)) = 3 and D is linearly equivalent to 2H. Since h^0(O_C(2H)) = 3 = h^0(P^1,O(2)) and pullback along h is injective, H^0(O_C(2H)) = h^*H^0(P^1,O(2)). The pencil V := gamma^*H^0(P^1,O(1)) is therefore h^*V' for a 2-dimensional V' inside H^0(P^1,O(2)), and V base-point-free forces V' base-point-free, so V' defines a degree-2 morphism beta : P^1 -> P^1 with gamma = beta o h (up to an automorphism of the target). Now\n\n  gamma_*O_C = beta_*(h_*O_C) = beta_*(O + O(-7)),\n\nusing h_*O_C = O + O(-(g+1)) = O + O(-7) for a genus-6 hyperelliptic double cover (branch divisor of degree 2g+2 = 14). For a degree-2 map beta one has beta_*O = O + O(-1) (rank 2, chi = 1). For beta_*O(-7): it is locally free of rank 2 with chi = chi(O_{P^1}(-7)) = -6, hence degree -8; and h^0(beta_*O(-7) tensor O(m)) = h^0(P^1, O(2m-7)), which vanishes for m = 3 and equals 2 for m = 4. Writing beta_*O(-7) = O(c_1) + O(c_2): vanishing at m = 3 gives c_i <= -4, and the value 2 at m = 4 forces c_1 = c_2 = -4. Therefore\n\n  gamma_*O_C = O + O(-1) + O(-4) + O(-4),\n\nand by uniqueness of splitting types on P^1 (Krull-Schmidt / Grothendieck),\n\n  **(2.2)  E^vee = O(-1) + O(-4) + O(-4),  i.e.  E = O(1) + O(4) + O(4).**\n\n*Consistency checks.* deg E = 9 = g+3 as it must be for a degree-4 cover; and h^0(gamma_*O_C tensor O(m)) = h^0(C, gamma^*O(m)) = h^0(2mH) reproduces 3, 5, 11 for m = 1, 2, 4 from both sides.\n\n### 3. Step 3: the hyperelliptic splitting type is not balanced\n\n**(3a) The conic-bundle exact sequence.** Twisting the CE resolution (R) by O_P(2) and splitting it into two short exact sequences, the image of pi^*F tensor O_P(-2) -> O_P is the ideal sheaf I_C, so\n\n  0 -> pi^*(det E) tensor O_P(-2) -> pi^*F -> I_C tensor O_P(2) -> 0.\n\nSince R^i pi_*O_P(-2) = 0 for every i on a P^2-bundle, applying pi_* gives F = pi_*pi^*F = pi_*(I_C tensor O_P(2)). Applying pi_* to 0 -> I_C(2) -> O_P(2) -> O_P(2)|_C -> 0 and using pi_*O_P(2) = Sym^2 E together with O_P(2)|_C = omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4) (by (N)), we obtain the left-exact sequence on P^1\n\n  **(3.1)  0 -> F -> Sym^2 E -> gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4)).**\n\n(Only left-exactness is used, so no claim about the four points of a fibre imposing independent conditions on conics is needed.)\n\n**(3b) The computation in the hyperelliptic case.** Assume C hyperelliptic, so E = O(1)+O(4)+O(4) by (2.2) and D is linearly equivalent to 2H, omega_C = 5H (as divisor classes, with h^0(mH) = m+1 for 0 <= m <= 5 and h^0(mH) = h^0(P^1,O(m)) + h^0(P^1,O(m-7)) in general). Then\n\n  Sym^2 E = O(2) + O(5) + O(5) + O(8) + O(8) + O(8),\n\nso Sym^2 E(-6) = O(-4) + O(-1)^2 + O(2)^3 and\n\n  **(3.2)  h^0(Sym^2 E(-6)) = 0 + 0 + 3.3 = 9.**\n\nOn the other side, gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4)) tensor O(-6) = gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(-2)), and since gamma is finite,\n\n  h^0(P^1, gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(-2))) = h^0(C, omega_C^2(-2D)) = h^0(10H - 4H) = h^0(6H) = 7 + 0 = 7,\n\nusing h^0(6H) = h^0(P^1,O(6)) + h^0(P^1,O(-1)) = 7. Hence\n\n  **(3.3)  h^0 of the target of (3.1) twisted by O(-6) equals 7.**\n\nTwisting (3.1) by O(-6) (twisting by a line bundle preserves exactness) and taking global sections,\n\n  **(3.4)  h^0(F(-6)) >= h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) - h^0(gamma_*(omega_C^2(-2D))) = 9 - 7 = 2 > 0.**\n\n**(3c) Conclusion.** If F were isomorphic to O(4)+O(5) then F(-6) = O(-2)+O(-1) and h^0(F(-6)) = 0, contradicting (3.4). Therefore\n\n  **(3.5)  C hyperelliptic implies F is not isomorphic to O(4)+O(5), i.e. (C,gamma) is not balanced.**\n\n*Independent cross-check (not load-bearing).* Writing F = O(f_1)+O(f_2), (3.4) gives max(0,f_1-5) + max(0,f_2-5) >= 2, so max(f_i) >= 7. The same computation with h^1 confirms this: h^1(Sym^2E(-6)) = 3, h^1 of the twisted target is 0, and (3.1) extended one term to the right forces h^1(F(-6)) >= 3, i.e. min(f_i) <= 2. Both routes agree that the hyperelliptic splitting type is strongly unbalanced.\n\n### 4. Step 4: the corank of the CE evaluation is at most 1, and the deeper strata are empty\n\nLet (C,gamma) be balanced and Gamma an effective divisor of degree 6 on C (marked points; n = 6). Fibrewise, the balanced condition splits W = W_4 + W_5; but *no* global splitting is used anywhere in this artifact: over a base only the canonical filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 of ATT-0048 CLM-0048-6 is used, and on a single geometric fibre the filtration argument below is carried out verbatim without splitting.\n\n**(4a) Ranks.** deg W_4 = 20 > 10 = 2g-2 and deg W_5 = 16 > 10, so H^1(W_4) = H^1(W_5) = 0 and, by the filtration, H^1(W) = 0. Riemann-Roch gives h^0(W_4) = 20-5 = 15 and h^0(W_5) = 16-5 = 11, so h^0(W) = 26; and h^0(W|_Gamma) = 2.6 = 12 since W has rank 2 and Gamma has length 6. Thus phi_Gamma : H^0(W) -> H^0(W|_Gamma) is a map of vector spaces of dimensions 26 -> 12, matching the packet's 26 -> 12 bookkeeping and ATT-0048 CLM-0048-7.\n\n**(4b) The f = 4 summand is surjective.** From 0 -> W_4(-Gamma) -> W_4 -> W_4|_Gamma -> 0 and H^1(W_4) = 0,\n\n  coker(phi_4) = H^1(W_4(-Gamma)),  deg W_4(-Gamma) = 20 - 6 = 14 > 10 = 2g-2,\n\nso coker(phi_4) = 0. (Equivalently, by Serre duality, coker(phi_4)^vee = H^0(omega_C tensor W_4^vee(Gamma)), a line bundle of degree 10 - 20 + 6 = -4 < 0, hence 0.)\n\n**(4c) The f = 5 summand has corank at most 1.** Similarly coker(phi_5) = H^1(W_5(-Gamma)) with deg W_5(-Gamma) = 16 - 6 = 10 = 2g-2. By Serre duality coker(phi_5)^vee = H^0(omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma)), a line bundle of degree 10 - 16 + 6 = 0. A degree-zero line bundle on a curve has a nonzero section if and only if it is trivial (a nonzero section has effective vanishing divisor of degree 0, hence is nowhere vanishing; no duality is needed for this step), and triviality of omega_C tensor W_5^vee(Gamma) is equivalent to O_C(Gamma) being isomorphic to W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = L. Hence\n\n  **(4.1)  dim coker(phi_5) = 1 if O_C(Gamma) is isomorphic to L, and 0 otherwise.**\n\n**(4d) Transfer to phi via the filtration.** The filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 restricts to an exact sequence on Gamma (a sequence of vector bundles stays exact after restriction to any subscheme) and stays exact after H^0 on the finite scheme Gamma; on C it stays exact after H^0 because H^1(W_4) = 0. The snake lemma applied to (phi_4, phi, phi_5) gives coker(phi_4) -> coker(phi) -> coker(phi_5) -> 0 exact; with coker(phi_4) = 0 this yields\n\n  **(4.2)  coker(phi_Gamma) is isomorphic to coker(phi_5), of dimension <= 1.**\n\nSo rk(phi_Gamma) is 12 or 11, and the corank is exactly 1 precisely on the Abel-Jacobi condition O_C(Gamma) isomorphic to L. This reproves fibrewise, without splitting, the identity that ATT-0048 CLM-0048-3 proves relatively and scheme-theoretically.\n\n**(4e) Determinantal ideals over a base.** Let B be a locally Noetherian base carrying a balanced family with a relative degree-6 divisor Gamma satisfying the hypotheses of ATT-0048 (Gamma a sum of 6 disjoint sections). Then phi : p_*W -> p_*(W|_Gamma) is a map of locally free sheaves of ranks 26 -> 12 whose formation commutes with arbitrary base change (all the R^1 vanishings of (4a) hold fibrewise, so cohomology and base change applies), and:\n\n  * Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi) (maximal minors), by ATT-0048 CLM-0048-7 / (T1);\n  * Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5) = I_6(phi_5), by ATT-0048 CLM-0048-3 (the filtered snake identity, whose fibrewise content is (4.2) above); note that this is an identity of ideals obtained by a filtered argument and *not* by a block-diagonal factorisation of minors, so it does not require the global splitting that the working context forbids;\n  * **I_11(phi) = O_B.** Indeed V(I_11(phi)) is the locus where rk(phi) <= 10, which is empty by (4.2) since the corank never exceeds 1; an ideal sheaf with empty vanishing locus is the unit ideal.\n\nThus the determinantal stratification of the CE evaluation at (g,n) = (6,6) on the balanced locus has exactly two strata, corank 0 and corank 1, and every deeper stratum is empty. On the corank-1 locus, coker(phi) is a cyclic module of fibre dimension 1.\n\n### 5. Step 5: the fibre dimension is constantly 2 on the balanced locus\n\nCombining (2.1), (3.5) and (1.2):\n\n  **(5.1)  If (C,gamma) is balanced then C is not hyperelliptic, hence h^0(O_C(D)) = 2, hence h^0(L) = 3 and h^1(L) = 2, and dim |L| = 2.**\n\nThis is the exact statement whose absence FND-0058 records: the identification of the CE evaluation-failure locus with a relative P^2 of fibre dimension exactly 2 \"requires proof that h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 1 + h^0(D) is constantly 3 on the whole balanced locus, not merely for general tetragonal curves\". (5.1) supplies that proof, and it does so *for every* balanced pair, with no genericity, Petri, or Brill-Noether-general hypothesis: the only way h^0(D) can jump is on hyperelliptic curves, and balancedness excludes those outright.\n\n### 6. Step 6: the relative statement -- a Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle\n\nLet B be a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme carrying a family p : C_B -> B of genus-6 curves with gamma_B : C_B -> P^1_B (or, more generally, to a P^1-bundle over B) finite flat of degree 4, balanced on every geometric fibre.\n\n**(6a) The twist ambiguity, and why it is harmless.** The extended working context correctly warns that F need not split globally, that the Harder-Narasimhan sub/quotient is not a direct sum, and that the relative O_{P^1}(-f) is defined only up to a twist by Pic(B); consequently W_5 and L are well defined only up to tensoring by p^*M for an invertible M on B. Two observations neutralise this for the present statement. First, the class of L in the *relative* Picard functor Pic_{C_B/B} = Pic(C_B)/p^*Pic(B) is canonical, so the classifying morphism a_L : B -> Pic^6_{C_B/B} is well defined. Second, replacing L by L tensor p^*M replaces p_*L by (p_*L) tensor M, which leaves the projectivisation P(p_*L) unchanged. So both objects appearing below are canonical.\n\n**(6b) Local freeness and base change.** By (5.1), h^0(L_b) = 3 and h^1(L_b) = 2 for every geometric point b of B (these numbers are unaffected by twisting L_b by a 1-dimensional vector space). Since B is reduced and h^0 is constant, Grauert's theorem gives: p_*L_B is locally free of rank 3 and its formation commutes with arbitrary base change; R^1p_*L_B is then locally free of rank 2, its formation likewise commuting with base change (top-degree base change for a relative curve holds unconditionally). This is precisely the hypothesis that the working-context constraints on Proj(p_*L) demand and that was previously unavailable: the identification of the relative linear system with the projectivisation is *licensed* here by a proof of constancy, not assumed in its absence.\n\n**(6c) The P^2-bundle.** Let AJ_B := Sym^6(C_B/B) x_{Pic^6_{C_B/B}, a_L} B be the relative Abel-Jacobi incidence (a closed subscheme of Sym^6(C_B/B), the relative Picard being separated). For every locally Noetherian B-scheme T, both AJ_B(T) and P(p_*L_B)(T) are canonically the set of relative effective Cartier divisors Gamma inside C_T, of degree 6 and flat over T, with O(Gamma) isomorphic to L_T tensor p_T^*M for some invertible M on T: a T-point of P(p_*L_B) is a line subbundle of (p_*L_B)_T with locally free quotient, i.e. (by (6b)) a section of L_T tensor p_T^*M up to scalar, and its divisor of zeros is such a Gamma; conversely such a Gamma yields the corresponding line subbundle. Hence\n\n  **(6.1)  AJ_B is canonically isomorphic to P(p_*L_B), a Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle over B.**\n\nIn particular AJ_B -> B is smooth and proper of pure relative dimension 2; AJ_B is irreducible if B is; and dim AJ_B = dim B + 2 on every irreducible component of B. Note that the smoothness asserted here is smoothness *of the projective bundle over B*, deduced from the bundle structure, and is not deduced from any dimension count.\n\n**(6d) Compatibility with the scheme-theoretic Z_ev of ATT-0048.** Over the ordered pairwise-distinct locus (where Gamma is a sum of 6 disjoint sections, so ATT-0048's hypotheses hold), ATT-0048 CLM-0048-4/CLM-0048-7 identify the Fitting/determinantal scheme Z_ev with a_N^{-1}(e(B)), i.e. with the scheme-theoretic Abel-Jacobi incidence for N = omega tensor W_5^vee(Gamma); pulled back along the ordering map, that is exactly the restriction of AJ_B to the reduced-support ordered locus. Hence the fibre-dimension and purity conclusions of (6.1) transfer to Z_ev there, now as statements about a *scheme-theoretically* identified locus rather than a set-theoretic one. Nothing is claimed about Z_ev over non-reduced Gamma, where ATT-0048's disjoint-sections hypothesis does not apply.\n\n**(6e) Where the excess dimension comes from.** By (4e), on the corank-1 locus the CE evaluation is a corank-1 degeneracy of the 6-by-11 map phi_5, whose generic determinantal expectation is codimension (6-5)(11-5) = 6 in the ambient relative space; the ambient relative space Sym^6(C_B/B) -> B has relative dimension 6, so the expected relative fibre dimension of the degeneracy locus is 6 - 6 = 0. The actual relative fibre dimension is dim |L| = h^1(L) = 2 by (5.1). So\n\n  **(6.2)  excess (actual minus expected relative fibre dimension) = h^1(L) = 2, exactly.**\n\nThis identifies the source of the excess: since chi(L) = 1, the Abel-Jacobi fibre is a P^{h^1(L)} rather than a reduced point. I draw **no** local-complete-intersection conclusion from the discrepancy between expected codimension 6 and actual codimension 4; the working context records that a degeneracy locus of below-expected codimension may still be locally cut out by a regular sequence of its actual codimension, and no local depth or conormal computation is performed here.\n\n**(6f) A geometric restriction on the balanced locus.** By (3.5), the balanced locus contains no hyperelliptic curve. Since the hyperelliptic locus in M_6 has dimension 11 and carries a 2-dimensional family of base-point-free degree-4 pencils (the base-point-free 2-planes in the 3-dimensional H^0(2H), an open subset of Gr(2,3) = P^2), the corresponding 13-dimensional locus of tetragonal pairs is entirely excluded from the balanced base. This is one instance -- not the general case -- of the frontier obligation to exclude larger components over special curve loci; see the gap markers.\n\n### 7. Step 7: recovery of gamma from (C,Gamma), injectivity, and automorphisms\n\nLet (C,gamma) be balanced and Gamma an effective degree-6 divisor with O_C(Gamma) isomorphic to L, i.e. a point of the fibre of (6.1).\n\n**(7a) The residual pencil is complete and is the gamma-pencil.** From O_C(Gamma) = L = omega_C(-D) we get\n\n  omega_C(-Gamma) = omega_C tensor L^{-1} = O_C(D),\n\nso h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = h^0(O_C(D)) = 2 by (5.1). The 2-dimensional subspace gamma^*H^0(P^1,O(1)) of H^0(O_C(D)) is therefore the *whole* space; it is base-point-free (being the pullback of the very ample O(1) along a morphism). Hence:\n\n  **(7.1)  the complete linear system |omega_C(-Gamma)| is a base-point-free pencil and coincides with the pencil defining gamma; gamma is recovered from (C,Gamma) alone, uniquely up to the action of PGL_2 on the target.**\n\nThis is precisely the \"recovery of the tetragonal pencil from L\" half of the monomorphic-embedding obligation, and it is unconditional on the balanced locus.\n\n**(7b) Injectivity on geometric points.** Suppose (C,gamma,Gamma) and (C',gamma',Gamma') are two such data and sigma : C -> C' is an isomorphism with sigma(Gamma) = Gamma' (as divisors, or as ordered tuples in the Conf_6 model). Then sigma^*(omega_{C'}(-Gamma')) = omega_C(-Gamma), so sigma carries the complete pencil of (7.1) for (C,Gamma) to that for (C',Gamma'); hence there is a unique tau in PGL_2 with gamma' o sigma = tau o gamma, i.e. sigma is an isomorphism of the triples. So the classifying map on geometric points is injective on isomorphism classes, and the balanced datum (C,gamma) is a *property* of (C,Gamma), not extra structure.\n\n**(7c) Automorphisms.** Define Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) := {(sigma,tau) in Aut(C) x Aut(P^1) : gamma o sigma = tau o gamma, sigma(Gamma) = Gamma} and Aut(C,Gamma) := {sigma in Aut(C) : sigma(Gamma) = Gamma}. The forgetful map (sigma,tau) -> sigma is injective (gamma is surjective, so tau is determined by sigma) and surjective (given sigma preserving Gamma, sigma preserves omega_C(-Gamma) and hence its complete linear system, producing the required tau by (7.1)). Therefore\n\n  **(7.2)  Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) -> Aut(C,Gamma) is a group isomorphism.**\n\nThis supplies the stabilizer comparison that FND-0060 identifies as missing when a coarse branched-cover dimension count is promoted to a statement about stacks: on the balanced locus the automorphism groups of the source datum and of its image in M_{6,6} agree, so no stacky discrepancy arises there.\n\n**(7d) Functorial form and its limits.** Over a base T, the same construction runs: h^0(omega_{C_T/T}(-Gamma_T)) = 2 and h^1 = h^0(O(Gamma_T)) = 3 are constant, so p_*(omega_{C_T/T}(-Gamma_T)) is a rank-2 bundle commuting with base change, and gamma_T is recovered as the induced morphism to the associated P^1-bundle P(p_*(omega(-Gamma))) over T. This shows that the correct target for the recovered tetragonal structure is a P^1-bundle over the base, not a constant P^1_T -- exactly the \"well-defined source and target\" issue flagged in the obligation. Spelling out the source moduli stack of balanced tetragonal data with this target convention, and verifying full faithfulness on all T-points, is not done here (see gap markers): what is proved is (7.1), (7.2) and injectivity on geometric points.\n\n### 8. Step 8: image dimension and the codimension statement, stated as an implication\n\nLet B be as in Step 6 and let AJ_B^{red-supp} be the (open) reduced-support locus of AJ_B. By (7b) the classifying map AJ_B^{red-supp} -> M_{6,6} (after ordering, on the finite etale S_6-cover of the reduced-support open, at stack level) is injective on geometric points, and by (7.2) it induces isomorphisms on automorphism groups. Hence its image has dimension equal to dim AJ_B = dim B + 2, and\n\n  **(8.1)  codim of the image in M_{6,6} = 21 - (dim B + 2) = 19 - dim B.**\n\nIn particular the image has codimension 4 **if and only if** dim B = 15. I do **not** assert dim B = 15. The standard route to it -- the branch-point count b = 2g-2+2d = 18 and dim = b - 3 = 15 -- is blocked in the working context absent a simple-branching hypothesis, and in any case an unconditional dim B = 15 requires exactly the exclusion of larger components over special curve loci that remains an open frontier obligation (Step 6f excludes the hyperelliptic locus, but not others). What Step 6 and Step 7 contribute is that, *given* dim B, the fibre dimension of the incidence (2, unconditionally) and the generic fibre dimension of the classifying map (0, indeed the map is injective on points and on Isom groups) are now computed rather than assumed; the codimension statement is thereby reduced to the single remaining numerical input dim B = 15.\n\nNo claim is made that the image is closed in M_{6,6}, that it is pure, that it is smooth, or that any Gysin sequence applies to it.\n\n### 9. A corollary on base points of L, and why nonemptiness is still open\n\nFor balanced (C,gamma) and p in C: p is a base point of |L| if and only if h^0(L(-p)) = h^0(L) = 3, i.e. h^0(omega_C(-D-p)) = 3, i.e. (Riemann-Roch: h^0(D+p) - h^1(D+p) = 5 - 6 + 1 = 0, so h^1(D+p) = h^0(D+p)) if and only if h^0(O_C(D+p)) = 3, i.e. D+p is a g^2_5. Hence\n\n  **(9.1)  L is base-point-free if and only if C carries no g^2_5 of the form D + p.**\n\nSuch g^2_5's do occur: a smooth plane quintic has genus 6 and hyperplane class Hp with omega_C = 2Hp; its degree-4 pencils are |Hp - p| with h^0 = 2, so D = Hp - p, and L = omega_C(-D) = Hp + p, which has p as a base point (h^0(Hp+p) = 1 + h^0(Hp - p) = 3 = h^0(Hp)). So base-point-freeness of L cannot be asserted on the whole balanced locus, and consequently the existence of a *reduced* member of |L| -- hence nonemptiness of the reduced-support open and of the ordered Conf_6 incidence -- does not follow from the results proved here. That obligation (and the corresponding exclusion recorded by ATT-0048 and by FND-0083) remains open; the present artifact neither asserts nor uses it.\n\n### 10. Separation of status\n\n**Proved here (no undischarged dependency).** Parts (A)-(H) of the theorem: the identification W_5 = omega_C^2(-D) and L = omega_C(-D) (Step 0, discharging GAP-Z-W5D from the weak normalisation plus the SRC-0004 degree receipt); the Riemann-Roch/Serre identity dim|L| = h^1(L) = h^0(O_C(D)) (Step 1); the dichotomy (2.1) including the converse (2d) (Step 2); the Tschirnhausen splitting type (2.2) in the hyperelliptic case (Step 2e); the exclusion (3.5) (Step 3); the corank bound and the emptiness of the deeper determinantal strata, including I_11(phi) = O_B (Step 4); the constancy (5.1) (Step 5); the P^2-bundle statement (6.1) over reduced locally Noetherian bases and the excess identity (6.2) (Step 6); pencil recovery (7.1), point-injectivity, and the automorphism isomorphism (7.2) (Step 7); the implication (8.1) (Step 8); and the base-point criterion (9.1) (Step 9).\n\n**Source-backed inputs.** SRC-0004 (Casnati-Ekedahl): the shape of the resolution (R) with rank-2 relations bundle F, the convention W_f = (O_P(2) tensor gamma^*O(-f))|_C, the degree formula deg(W_f|_C) = 4g+12-4f, and the normalisation receipt deg(O_P(1)|_C) = 18. Used in Step 0, Step 3a and Step 4a.\n\n**Dependency artifacts used (cited).** ATT-0048 (subproblem C66-GEO-Z), confirmed by REV-0091 and REV-0092: CLM-0048-6 (canonical Harder-Narasimhan filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 over a balanced family, no splitting), CLM-0048-3 (filtered snake identity Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5)), CLM-0048-7 (ranks 26 -> 12 and Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi) at (6,6)), CLM-0048-4/5 (scheme-theoretic Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) and its T-point criterion). These are used only in Step 4e and Step 6d; their fibrewise content is independently reproved in Steps 4a-4d, so Parts (A)-(D) on geometric fibres do not depend on ATT-0048.\n\n**Computational hypotheses.** None. No finite experiment supports any universal claim here: every numerical statement (degrees 20, 16, 6, 4, 0, -4; ranks 26, 12, 15, 11, 6; the splitting types Sym^2E = O(2)+O(5)^2+O(8)^3 and E = O(1)+O(4)^2; the counts 9, 7, 2) is either an arithmetic substitution reproved inline or a cohomological computation valid for every member of the stated class. The pinned reusable computations cited are arithmetic receipts only.\n\n**Explicitly not claimed.** (i) dim B = 15, hence the unconditional codimension 4; (ii) base-point-freeness of L, existence of a reduced member of |L|, nonemptiness of the reduced-support open or of the ordered Conf_6 incidence; (iii) anything about unbalanced splitting types, or about components of the evaluation-failure locus over special curve loci other than the hyperelliptic locus excluded in Step 6f; (iv) closedness of the image in M_{6,6}, purity of the full evaluation-failure locus in M_{6,6}, smoothness or lci-ness or any local structure of that image, and any Gysin sequence; (v) a full stack-level monomorphism on all T-points with the source moduli functor spelled out; (vi) any weight, Hodge-structure, purity-of-Hodge-type or Tate-type statement, in particular anything about W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) or W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q). Item (vi) is downstream (C66-TATE-SUPPORT, C66-TATE-ASSEMBLY) and no step of this argument is cohomological in the topological sense.\n\n### 11. Failed routes and constraints explicitly addressed\n\n* **\"The relative linear system |omega_C(-D)| is a relative P^2-bundle over the entire balanced tetragonal base\" (extended-context constraint; FND-0058).** The constraint's own reason states that h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 is available only under the hypothesis h^0(D) = 2, and warns about loci where h^0(D) jumps. This artifact does not assert the P^2-bundle in the absence of that hypothesis: it **proves the hypothesis** (Steps 2, 3, 5), showing that h^0(D) jumps only on hyperelliptic curves and that balancedness excludes those. The conclusion (6.1) is then licensed, not assumed.\n* **\"When h^0(L) may jump, identify the relative linear system with Proj(pi_*L)\" (primary and extended constraints).** Not walked. Constancy of h^0 is proved first; local freeness and base-change compatibility are then obtained from Grauert over a reduced base; and the identification in (6c) is made against the Abel-Jacobi incidence functor, not by fiat.\n* **\"The parameter count 15+2 = 17 proves generic finiteness onto a pure codimension-4 component\" (primary constraint).** Not used. The generic fibre dimension of the classifying map is computed (Step 7b: the map is injective on geometric points), the relative fibre dimension of the incidence is computed (Step 6: exactly 2, from a projective-bundle structure), the automorphism/stabilizer comparison is computed (7.2), and codimension 4 is stated only as the implication (8.1) conditional on dim B = 15.\n* **\"b - 3 = 15 without a simple-branching hypothesis\" (extended constraint).** Not used; dim B is never computed here and dim B = 15 is explicitly withheld.\n* **\"Smoothness of the 17-dimensional locus deduced from a dimension count\" (extended constraint).** Not used. The only smoothness asserted is smoothness of AJ_B -> B, which follows from the projective-bundle structure (6.1); no smoothness of any locus in M_{6,6} is claimed.\n* **\"Identify the balanced reduced-support relative linear system with a closed pure-codimension-4 subvariety Z in M_{6,6} and apply codimension-4 Gysin\" (extended constraint).** Not used. No closedness and no Gysin sequence appear.\n* **\"Infer a global splitting W = W_4 + W_5 from the fibrewise balanced condition\" (primary constraint).** Not used. Step 4d uses only the filtration and the snake lemma (fibrewise, reproved), and Step 4e cites ATT-0048's filtered identity; the block-diagonal minor factorisation is deliberately avoided.\n* **\"Quantify W_5 or L over the whole family rather than over geometric fibres\" (extended constraint).** Addressed head-on in (6a): L is well defined only up to a Pic(B)-twist, but the class a_L in the relative Picard and the projectivisation P(p_*L) are both invariant under that twist, so the objects used in (6.1) are canonical.\n* **\"From expected determinantal codimension 6 versus observed codimension 4, conclude nowhere-lci\" (primary constraint; FND-0068).** Not used. The two numbers are recorded and the excess is *explained* as h^1(L) = 2 in (6.2), and no lci or regular-sequence conclusion is drawn in either direction.\n* **\"Conclude non-closedness of the balanced piece from openness plus one balanced example\" (primary constraints).** Not used; no closedness or non-closedness statement about the balanced locus is made.\n* **\"Conclude nonemptiness of the reduced-support open or of Z_ev from chi(L) = 1\" (extended constraint; FND-0083).** Not used. Step 9 shows L need not even be base-point-free (plane quintics), and nonemptiness is explicitly listed as open.\n* **\"Identify the Fitting/determinantal scheme with the Abel-Jacobi incidence from agreement of geometric points\" (primary and extended constraints).** Not used. Where a scheme-theoretic identification is needed (Step 6d) it is imported from ATT-0048, which proves it by comparing ideals; where only fibre dimensions are needed (Steps 5, 6) the argument is a direct cohomological computation.\n* **\"Assert the S_6-torsor structure on coarse moduli\" and \"call the reduced-support locus closed\" (constraints).** Not used; the reduced-support locus is treated as open throughout and the ordering comparison is invoked only at stack level, exactly as the packet instructs.\n* **Blocked campaign routes** vcd-only-vanishing, undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex, point-count-without-degree-parity-separation, forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown: none is used. No cohomological-degree, weight, graph-complex, point-count or forgetful-map step occurs anywhere in this argument. Accordingly new_inputs is empty.\n\n### 12. What this advances\n\nThe frontier obligation \"Constancy of h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 on the balanced locus\" is discharged in full by (5.1), together with the sharper geometric statement that the balanced locus avoids the hyperelliptic locus entirely. The obligation \"Monomorphic embedding of the balanced model into M_{6,6}: recovery of the tetragonal pencil from L, a well-defined source and target, and stack-level full faithfulness covering automorphism groups\" is advanced by (7.1) (recovery), (7b) (injectivity on geometric points), (7.2) (automorphism groups), and (7d) (the correct P^1-bundle target); what remains is the moduli-functor bookkeeping for all T-points. The obligation \"Exhaustive component, dimension, stabilizer, closure, and codimension analysis of Z\" is advanced on the dimension, stabilizer and fibre-purity fronts -- (6.1), (6.2), (7.2) -- and reduced, on the codimension front, to the single input dim B = 15 by (8.1); the component-exhaustiveness and closure fronts remain open and are recorded as gap markers, as does the second half of the obligation (\"exclusion of larger components over special curve loci\"), of which only the hyperelliptic case is settled here. The clarification of the obligation's parenthetical \"equivalently exclusion of balanced non-primitive degree-4 pencils\" is recorded in GAP-RANK-PRIMITIVE below: the constancy statement is equivalent to excluding the *hyperelliptic-composed* covers only, and non-primitive covers factoring through an intermediate curve of genus 1, 2 or 3 have h^0(D) = 2 and are not excluded by -- and are not needed for -- the constancy conclusion.", "claims": [{"id": "CLM-0063-1", "statement": "For a degree-4 cover gamma : C -> P^1 of a genus-6 curve with the Casnati-Ekedahl normalisation O_{P(E^vee)}(1)|_C = omega_C tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(2), one has W_4 = omega_C^2 of degree 20, W_5 = omega_C^2(-D) of degree 16 and L := W_5 tensor omega_C^{-1} = omega_C(-D) of degree 6, and dim|L| = h^0(L) - 1 = h^1(L) = h^0(O_C(D)).", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 0: W_f := (O_P(2) tensor gamma^*O(-f))|_C = omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4-f) by the normalisation, which is itself forced from the weak form by the degree receipt deg(O_P(1)|_C) = 18 and Pic(P^1) = Z. Step 1: chi(L) = 6-6+1 = 1 gives h^0(L) = 1 + h^1(L), and Serre duality gives h^1(L) = h^0(omega_C tensor L^{-1}) = h^0(O_C(D)). This discharges the marker GAP-Z-W5D."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-2", "statement": "For every degree-4 cover gamma of a genus-6 curve C with D = gamma^*(pt), h^0(O_C(D)) is 2 or 3, and it equals 3 if and only if C is hyperelliptic; in that case D is linearly equivalent to 2H and gamma factors as beta o h with beta of degree 2.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 2. Lower bound: gamma^*H^0(O(1)) is 2-dimensional. D is special since h^1(D) = h^0(L) >= chi(L) = 1. Clifford gives h^0 <= 3, with equality only for D = 0, D = K or C hyperelliptic with D a multiple of the hyperelliptic class; deg D = 4 excludes the first two. Converse: if C is hyperelliptic and h^0(D) = 2, the map (h,gamma) : C -> P^1 x P^1 is either of degree 2 onto a rational image (forcing gamma to factor through h and h^0(D) = 3, a contradiction) or birational onto a curve of bidegree (4,2) with p_a = (4-1)(2-1) = 3 < 6 = g(C), also a contradiction."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-3", "statement": "If C is hyperelliptic of genus 6 and gamma has degree 4, then the Tschirnhausen bundle satisfies E = O(1) + O(4) + O(4), and consequently h^0(F(-6)) >= 2, so the Casnati-Ekedahl relations bundle F is not isomorphic to O(4) + O(5): no balanced tetragonal genus-6 curve is hyperelliptic.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 2e: gamma = beta o h gives gamma_*O_C = beta_*(O + O(-7)) = (O + O(-1)) + (O(-4) + O(-4)), where beta_*O(-7) = O(-4)^2 is pinned by h^0(beta_*O(-7) tensor O(m)) = h^0(O(2m-7)) at m = 3 and m = 4; uniqueness of splitting types on P^1 identifies E^vee. Step 3: pushing forward the O_P(2)-twist of the CE resolution gives left-exactness of 0 -> F -> Sym^2 E -> gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4)) (using R^i pi_* O_P(-2) = 0); twisting by O(-6), h^0(Sym^2E(-6)) = 9 and h^0(omega_C^2(-2D)) = h^0(6H) = 7, so h^0(F(-6)) >= 2, while O(4)+O(5) twisted by O(-6) has no sections."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-4", "statement": "If (C,gamma) is balanced (F isomorphic to O(4)+O(5)) then h^0(O_C(D)) = 2, h^0(L) = 3 and h^1(L) = 2; hence the residual linear system |L| is a P^2 for every balanced pair, and h^0(L) does not jump anywhere on the balanced locus.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Immediate from CLM-0063-1, CLM-0063-2 and CLM-0063-3 (Step 5): jumping of h^0(O_C(D)) occurs only on hyperelliptic curves, and balancedness excludes those. This is exactly the statement FND-0058 records as required and previously unavailable."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-5", "statement": "For balanced (C,gamma) and every effective degree-6 divisor Gamma on C, the CE evaluation phi_Gamma of ranks 26 -> 12 satisfies coker(phi_4) = 0 and coker(phi_Gamma) isomorphic to coker(phi_5) = H^1(W_5(-Gamma)), of dimension 1 if O_C(Gamma) is isomorphic to L and 0 otherwise; hence corank(phi_Gamma) <= 1 everywhere. Relatively over a base carrying such a family, Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi) = I_6(phi_5) while I_11(phi) is the unit ideal, i.e. the corank->=2 determinantal stratum is empty.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 4: deg W_4 = 20 > 10 and deg W_4(-Gamma) = 14 > 10 give H^1(W_4) = 0 and coker(phi_4) = H^1(W_4(-Gamma)) = 0; deg W_5(-Gamma) = 10 = 2g-2 makes coker(phi_5) Serre-dual to H^0 of a degree-zero line bundle, nonzero exactly on the Abel-Jacobi condition and then 1-dimensional; the snake lemma applied to the filtration (never to a splitting) transfers this to coker(phi). Emptiness of V(I_11(phi)) forces I_11(phi) = O_B. The relative Fitting identities are ATT-0048 CLM-0048-3 and CLM-0048-7 (confirmed by REV-0091, REV-0092), whose fibrewise content is reproved here."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-6", "statement": "Let B be a reduced locally Noetherian k-scheme carrying a balanced family. Then p_*L_B is locally free of rank 3 and R^1p_*L_B locally free of rank 2, both commuting with base change; the classifying map a_L : B -> Pic^6_{C_B/B} and the projectivisation P(p_*L_B) are canonical despite the Pic(B)-twist ambiguity of L_B; and the relative Abel-Jacobi incidence AJ_B is canonically isomorphic to the Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle P(p_*L_B), hence is smooth and proper over B of pure relative dimension 2 with dim AJ_B = dim B + 2 on every component.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 6: constancy h^0(L_b) = 3 (CLM-0063-4) plus reducedness of B gives Grauert local freeness and base change; top-degree base change gives R^1; the relative Picard quotients out p^*Pic(B) so a_L is canonical, and P(V tensor M) = P(V) makes the projectivisation canonical; AJ_B and P(p_*L_B) represent the same functor of relative effective degree-6 divisors with O(Gamma) relatively isomorphic to L. Over the ordered pairwise-distinct locus this restricts to the scheme-theoretic Z_ev of ATT-0048 CLM-0048-4/7."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-7", "statement": "For balanced (C,gamma) and Gamma in |L|, omega_C(-Gamma) = O_C(D) has h^0 = 2 and its complete linear system is a base-point-free pencil equal to the gamma-pencil; hence gamma is recovered from (C,Gamma) uniquely up to PGL_2 on the target, the classifying map is injective on isomorphism classes of geometric points, and Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) -> Aut(C,Gamma) is a group isomorphism. Consequently the image in M_{6,6} of the reduced-support open has dimension dim B + 2 and codimension 19 - dim B, which equals 4 if and only if dim B = 15.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 7 and Step 8. h^0(omega_C(-Gamma)) = h^0(O_C(D)) = 2 by CLM-0063-4, so the gamma-pencil exhausts the complete system; any isomorphism preserving (C,Gamma) preserves omega_C(-Gamma) and hence the pencil, giving the unique target automorphism. The codimension statement is an implication only: dim B = 15 is not asserted, the branch-count route to it being blocked absent a simple-branching hypothesis and its unconditional form requiring the still-open exclusion of larger components over special curve loci."}, {"id": "CLM-0063-8", "statement": "The excess of the actual relative fibre dimension of the evaluation-failure locus over the generic determinantal expectation equals h^1(L) = 2 exactly; and a point p of C is a base point of |L| if and only if h^0(O_C(D+p)) = 3, i.e. D+p is a g^2_5, which does occur (smooth plane quintics), so L is not base-point-free on the whole balanced locus.", "status": "proved", "justification": "Step 6e: corank-1 degeneracy of a 6-by-11 map has expected codimension (6-5)(11-5) = 6 inside a relative Sym^6 of relative dimension 6, hence expected relative fibre dimension 0, while the actual value is dim|L| = h^1(L) = 2 by CLM-0063-1 and CLM-0063-4. Step 9: p is a base point iff h^0(omega_C(-D-p)) = h^0(L) = 3, and Riemann-Roch (h^0(D+p) = h^1(D+p) since deg(D+p) = 5 and chi = 0) converts this to h^0(O_C(D+p)) = 3; on a smooth plane quintic with hyperplane class Hp, D = Hp - p and L = Hp + p has p as a base point. No lci or nonemptiness conclusion is drawn from either statement."}], "proof_dependencies": ["SRC-0004", "ATT-0048"], "experiment_ids": [], "experiment_uses": [], "novelty_claims": ["First proof that the balanced Casnati-Ekedahl splitting type F = O(4)+O(5) forces h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 at every point of the balanced tetragonal genus-6 locus, discharging in full the requirement recorded by FND-0058 and the corresponding frontier obligation; the previous status was that the P^2-bundle description of the incidence was available only under an unproved constancy hypothesis.", "The sharper geometric statement that a balanced tetragonal genus-6 curve is never hyperelliptic, proved by computing the Tschirnhausen bundle E = O(1)+O(4)+O(4) of a hyperelliptic degree-4 cover and deducing h^0(F(-6)) >= 2 from the conic-bundle sequence 0 -> F -> Sym^2E -> gamma_*(omega_C^2 tensor gamma^*O(4)); this removes the entire 13-dimensional hyperelliptic tetragonal locus from the balanced base.", "The converse half of the h^0 dichotomy: a hyperelliptic genus-6 curve admits no base-point-free degree-4 pencil with h^0 = 2, proved by a bidegree/adjunction argument on P^1 x P^1 (a birational image would have arithmetic genus (4-1)(2-1) = 3 < 6). This makes the dichotomy h^0(O_C(D)) in {2,3} an exact characterisation of hyperellipticity rather than a one-sided Clifford bound.", "Determinantal rank-drop computation: the corank of the 26 -> 12 CE evaluation at (g,n) = (6,6) never exceeds 1, so the determinantal stratification has exactly two strata and I_11(phi) is the unit ideal; the corank->=2 locus is empty. This is a structural statement about the Fitting stratification that neither ATT-0048 nor the packet records.", "Identification of the excess dimension: the discrepancy between the generic determinantal expectation and the actual incidence dimension is exactly h^1(L) = 2, because chi(L) = 1 makes the Abel-Jacobi fibre a P^{h^1(L)}. This explains the expected-6-versus-actual-4 codimension gap without drawing any lci conclusion.", "Recovery of the tetragonal pencil from the marked divisor: for balanced data, |omega_C(-Gamma)| is a complete base-point-free pencil equal to the gamma-pencil, giving injectivity on geometric points and a group isomorphism Aut(C,gamma,Gamma) = Aut(C,Gamma). This supplies both the pencil-recovery half of the monomorphic-embedding obligation and the stabilizer comparison FND-0060 identifies as missing, and it identifies the correct functorial target as a P^1-bundle rather than a constant P^1.", "The observation that L = omega_C(-D) is base-point-free exactly when C carries no g^2_5 of the form D+p, together with the plane-quintic counterexample; this constrains the still-open nonemptiness obligation by showing the naive route through base-point-freeness plus Bertini cannot be uniform over the balanced locus.", "Correction of the frontier obligation's parenthetical equivalence: constancy of h^0(omega_C(-D)) = 3 is equivalent to excluding hyperelliptic-composed degree-4 pencils only, not all non-primitive ones; covers factoring through an intermediate curve of genus 1, 2 or 3 satisfy h^0(D) = 2 and are neither excluded by, nor needed for, the constancy conclusion."], "gap_markers": [{"id": "GAP-RANK-DIMB", "description": "dim B = 15 for the balanced tetragonal base is not proved here, so the codimension-4 statement in M_{6,6} is delivered only as the implication codim = 19 - dim B. The branch-point route b - 3 = 15 is blocked absent a simple-branching hypothesis, and an unconditional value requires excluding larger components over special curve loci (only the hyperelliptic locus is excluded here).", "severity": "open_dependency"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-COMPONENTS", "description": "Exhaustive component analysis of the evaluation-failure locus is not performed. Step 6f excludes the hyperelliptic locus from the balanced base, but no claim is made about other special curve loci (for instance bielliptic, plane-quintic, or trigonal-adjacent loci), about unbalanced splitting types, or about closure behaviour of the balanced piece inside the full locus. Non-closedness of the balanced piece is neither claimed nor disclaimed.", "severity": "open_obligation"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-BPF", "description": "Base-point-freeness of L and the existence of a reduced member of |L| are not established; Step 9 shows L can fail to be base-point-free (plane quintics). Consequently nonemptiness of the reduced-support open and of the ordered Conf_6 incidence on every geometric fibre remains open, exactly as excluded by ATT-0048 and recorded by FND-0083.", "severity": "open_obligation"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-STACK", "description": "Step 7 proves injectivity on geometric points and an isomorphism of automorphism groups, and Step 7d gives the functorial recovery with a P^1-bundle target, but the source moduli stack of balanced tetragonal data is not spelled out and full faithfulness on all T-points is not verified. The monomorphic-embedding obligation is therefore advanced, not discharged.", "severity": "partial"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-LOCALSTRUCTURE", "description": "No local structure of the image in M_{6,6} is established: no smoothness, no immersion property, no lci or regular-sequence statement, no conormal or depth computation, and no Gysin sequence. The recorded discrepancy between expected codimension 6 and actual codimension 4 is explained as excess h^1(L) = 2 but yields no conclusion about local complete intersection status in either direction.", "severity": "not_claimed"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-PRIMITIVE", "description": "The frontier obligation phrases the constancy statement as 'equivalently exclusion of balanced non-primitive degree-4 pencils'. That equivalence is not literally correct and is not used: h^0(O_C(D)) = 3 characterises exactly the covers that factor through the hyperelliptic map (intermediate curve of genus 0). A degree-4 cover factoring through a degree-2 cover of an intermediate curve of genus 1, 2 or 3 has h^0(O_C(D)) = 2 and is not excluded here; whether such covers can be balanced is left open, and the constancy conclusion does not depend on it.", "severity": "scope_clarification"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-NONREDUCED-BASE", "description": "The relative statement of Step 6 assumes the base B is reduced (Grauert). Over a non-reduced locally Noetherian base the constancy of h^0 and h^1 on fibres does not by itself give local freeness, and a separate cohomology-and-base-change argument would be required. The dimension conclusions are unaffected, but the P^2-bundle statement as written is scoped to reduced bases.", "severity": "scope_restriction"}, {"id": "GAP-RANK-HODGE", "description": "Nothing in this artifact concerns weights, mixed Hodge structures, purity of Hodge type, or Tate type; in particular no statement is made or implied about W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) or W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q). Those belong to C66-TATE-SUPPORT and C66-TATE-ASSEMBLY and are downstream of C66-GEO-H0 and C66-GEO-COMP.", "severity": "not_claimed"}], "failed_approaches_addressed": [{"disposition": "", "kind": 